      "claim": "Nevirapine has an absolute oral bioavailability of 91 ± 8% in healthy volunteers, supporting high F for a moderately lipophilic drug with good absorption.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C21"],
      "claim": "Bicifadine, a bridged bicyclic amine, has an absolute oral bioavailability of approximately 67% in humans, providing a structurally closer amine-containing analogue with F well above 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "Pentazocine has an absolute oral bioavailability of 18% in healthy subjects, below the threshold, but its low F is attributed to phenolic first-pass metabolism that is not present in the query molecule.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a small lipophilic adamantane primary amine with no acidic, phenolic, or other obvious first-pass conjugation liabilities. High-F analogues with strong transfer scores—ethosuximide (>90%), cilomilast (~100%), nevirapine (~91%), and bicifadine (~67%)—support F ≥20%. The only sub-threshold analogue, pentazocine (18%), owes its low F to phenolic first-pass metabolism absent in the query. Therefore predict high.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute bioavailability measurement for the query molecule is present in the supplied evidence.",
    "No adamantane-containing analogue with a measured F value is available; extrapolation relies on small lipophilic drugs with high oral bioavailability."
  ],
